WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:04.560
An actor might be replaced in a television&nbsp;
series due to schedule conflicts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.900
lackluster chemistry, behind-the-scenes politics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.380
or even death. Forgotten or not,&nbsp;
there’s drama behind the curtain!

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.120
It's New York City in the early '80s.&nbsp;
Christine Cagney is a modern single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.780
woman and a former fashion model, raised&nbsp;
in a comfortable middle-class home. Mary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.760
Beth Lacey is a no-nonsense working mom from the&nbsp;
neighborhood. Together they are beat-stylin’ cops.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.200
They patrol the mean streets of Midtown Manhattan&nbsp;
while fighting against institutional sexism in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.985
their own ranks. Cagney and Lacey had a passionate&nbsp;
fan base that twice saved it from cancellation.

00:00:33.106 --> 00:00:35.599
"These your women? Macy and–"

00:00:35.599 --> 00:00:36.787
"Lacey, sir."

00:00:36.787 --> 00:00:38.608
"Cagney, Inspector."

00:00:38.608 --> 00:00:43.020
Stars Tyne Daly and Sharon Gless dominated&nbsp;
the Lead Actress Emmy category for the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.740
six-year run of the series. But Gless&nbsp;
wasn't the first actress cast as Cagney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:51.540
or even the second. The series began with a TV&nbsp;
movie starring Daly and M*A*S*H* star Loretta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:56.100
Swit. When Swit was unavailable for the show,&nbsp;
Meg Foster was cast in her place. But after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.140
six episodes, Gless took over the part. In a&nbsp;
wide-ranging 2008 interview, series producer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.160
Barney Rosenzweig revealed the reason for&nbsp;
Foster's departure was one of compatibility:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.540
her performance was too similar to what&nbsp;
Daly was creating as Lacey. He explained,

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.340
"It became Lacey and Lacey."

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.180
At the time of the recasting, however,&nbsp;
Rosenzweig gave a much different rationale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.860
when interviewed by TV Guide: that viewers found&nbsp;
Foster not feminine enough, and that together she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.830
and Daly read as lesbians — an apparently&nbsp;
distasteful situation to viewers in 1982.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:27.360
"If you have a problem, if no one else can help,

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.020
and if you can find them,&nbsp;
maybe YOU can hire the A-Team!"

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.820
So goes the famous opening narration of The&nbsp;
A-Team, super-producer Stephen J. Cannell's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:39.900
1980s action series about a quartet of&nbsp;
Vietnam vets who work as mercenaries in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.980
the Los Angeles underground, taking the kinds&nbsp;
of suicide missions that no one else is bold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:48.900
or crazy enough to accept. The series stars '60s&nbsp;
leading man George Peppard as "Hannibal" Smith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:53.220
the leader of the group, Dwight Schultz&nbsp;
as pilot "Howlin' Mad" Murdock, Rocky III&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.840
breakout star Mr. T as all B.A. Baracus –&nbsp;
the B.A. stands for "Bad Attitude" -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.980
Battlestar Galactica star Dirk Benedict&nbsp;
as master of disguise, "Faceman" Peck.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.880
But the first time around, "Faceman" had&nbsp;
a different face altogether. In the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.020
pilot episode, Peck is played by Love Boat&nbsp;
star Tim Dunigan. The issue with Dunigan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:14.340
appropriately enough, was his face. Though&nbsp;
technically old enough to have served in Vietnam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.660
it was decided that, in Dunigan's own words,&nbsp;
he was "too young and too tall" for the role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.380
Benedict, who apparently had been Cannell&nbsp;
and co-creator Frank Lupo's first choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.104
was cast and played the part&nbsp;
for the rest of the series.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:29.940
The Law &amp; Order franchise is no stranger&nbsp;
to inventive solutions for temporarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:34.680
unavailable cast members. Law &amp; Order:&nbsp;
Special Victims Unit premiered in 1999,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.820
the first franchise spinoff. The series&nbsp;
follows the dedicated detectives of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.840
sex crimes-focused Special Victims Unit and&nbsp;
delves further into the personal lives of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:47.280
its leads than the original show ever does.&nbsp;
This is especially true for Mariska Hargitay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.840
as Detective Olivia Benson and Christopher&nbsp;
Meloni as her volatile partner, Elliot Stabler.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.160
In 2006, it was announced that Hargitay was&nbsp;
taking a short leave of absence from the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.660
to accommodate the final months of her pregnancy.&nbsp;
In her place, the show brought in Connie Nielsen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.680
as Detective Dani Beck, partnering her with&nbsp;
Stabler for a six-episode run in the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.720
of Season 8. Her character was clearly meant to&nbsp;
be temporary. Still, some fans weren't pleased by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:14.160
her presence. In a 2020 interview, Nielsen&nbsp;
said she still gets Dani Beck hate mail.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.220
Tony Shaloub's "defective detective" in the comedy&nbsp;
series Monk was always socially awkward with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:24.900
touch of obsessive-compulsive disorder. Various&nbsp;
flashbacks through the seasons and a visit from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.560
his brother confirm that his conditions were&nbsp;
always present within him. But the moment that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.160
turned his life upside down to the point that he&nbsp;
had to leave the San Francisco Police Department&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.260
and hire a nurse to assist him was his wife’s&nbsp;
death. His wife, Trudy, was murdered by car bomb.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:40.740
Monk begins after Trudy's death,&nbsp;
but the mystery surrounding it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.680
the show's primary ongoing story,&nbsp;
reaching from the first episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.960
all the way to the last. In the first two&nbsp;
seasons, Trudy, played by Stellina Rusich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.740
is represented by a few silent flashbacks&nbsp;
and photographs on Monk's mantle.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.260
Starting with Season 3's flashback-heavy episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.700
"Mr. Monk and the Game Show," however, Trudy&nbsp;
is played in flashbacks by Melora Hardin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.940
an actress probably best known for&nbsp;
playing Jan Levinson in The Office.

00:04:03.601 --> 00:04:09.181
"I have to thank you for that other thing,&nbsp;
too. You know. Marrying me. Oh look."

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:11.722
"You're welcome."

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.500
Hardin would appear a few more times, most&nbsp;
notably in the two-part series finale where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:21.360
her murder is finally solved. Oddly, as the series&nbsp;
went on, the props department used photos of both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:25.924
Rusich and Hardin interchangeably, and even&nbsp;
sat them next to each other in Monk's house.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:30.900
Generational trauma is at the heart of&nbsp;
David Chase's landmark series The Sopranos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.860
the HBO mob drama that in many ways&nbsp;
revolutionized television, opening the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:40.140
medium up to a more mature, serialized mode of&nbsp;
storytelling. Tony Soprano is a New Jersey mob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:44.520
boss who is profoundly depressed and dissatisfied&nbsp;
with his life in ways that he can't articulate.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.120
Much of his trauma can be traced back&nbsp;
to his mother Livia and Uncle Junior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:52.020
as well as the ghost of his father,&nbsp;
Johnny Boy. Beginning with Season 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:56.580
Tony's wayward sister Janice arrives on the scene.&nbsp;
She's dealt with their tumultuous upbringing by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:00.960
moving west and embracing new age philosophies,&nbsp;
but can't help but get drawn back into their life.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.480
Tony and Janice aren't the only Soprano siblings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:07.740
however. There's one more — a little&nbsp;
sister who got out and stayed out, Barbara,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.200
played in a handful of episodes&nbsp;
in Season 2 by Nicole Burdette.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:14.880
Barbara lives out of town and is rarely involved&nbsp;
with the Soprano family business. The character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.920
is absent from Season 4, then returns for&nbsp;
ten episodes in the show's last two seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.840
this time played by Danielle Di Vecchio. Even with&nbsp;
her increased presence in the series' final run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:28.920
Barbara was never a major character, and her&nbsp;
role was by far the smallest in the family.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.360
NBC's Hannibal, based on the&nbsp;
bestselling novels by Thomas Harris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.140
was unlike any show on network&nbsp;
television. Baroque, gorgeous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:41.400
and gruesome, series creator Bryan Fuller&nbsp;
crafted a bloody thriller for three seasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.763
that was beautiful to watch, even if&nbsp;
you were watching through your fingers.

00:05:45.202 --> 00:05:48.622
"This is the nightmare that&nbsp;
followed him out of his dreams."

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.860
Centered around haunted FBI profiler&nbsp;
Will Graham and his psychiatrist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:57.480
the erudite gourmet Hannibal Lecter, the series&nbsp;
follows Graham's attempts to catch various serial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:01.560
killers and protect his own fragile sanity,&nbsp;
while Lecter works to keep Graham and his FBI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:05.400
boss Jack Crawford from discovering that he&nbsp;
is a brutal, cannibalistic killer himself.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.580
Season 2 works in elements&nbsp;
from Harris's sequel, Hannibal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:13.260
introducing meatpacking heiress Margot Verger&nbsp;
and her sadistic, incestuous brother Mason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:18.120
played by Michael Pitt. Hannibal punishes&nbsp;
Mason for his abuse of Margot and for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:23.220
general impoliteness by drugging him and forcing&nbsp;
him to mutilate and eat his own face. In Season 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:27.600
Mason returns for revenge, paralyzed from&nbsp;
the neck down and horribly disfigured.

00:06:28.126 --> 00:06:33.406
"I would like you to begin arrangements&nbsp;
for Dr. Hannibal Lecter to be eaten alive."

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:37.440
But the actor underneath the prosthetics&nbsp;
is not Pitt, but actor Joe Anderson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:40.324
as Pitt declined to return&nbsp;
to the role after Season 2.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:45.840
Premiering on NBC the same year as Hannibal with&nbsp;
a somewhat similar premise was The Blacklist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:51.120
James Spader stars as Raymond "Red" Reddington, a&nbsp;
master criminal who's evaded capture for decades.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.140
Reddington turns himself in to the FBI&nbsp;
in exchange for immunity, promising to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:59.040
help the Bureau track down the world's most&nbsp;
dangerous criminals: his "blacklist." But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.480
of course, there's a catch. Reddington will&nbsp;
only work with rookie profiler Elizabeth Keen.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:07.200
While the series has a "case of the week"&nbsp;
structure with Red and Keen bringing down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:10.620
one illegal operation or another, the&nbsp;
central mystery of Reddington's true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:14.580
identity and his connection to Liz power&nbsp;
the show through ten high-intrigue seasons.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:17.700
A major clue to the mystery of&nbsp;
both Red and Liz's backgrounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:20.940
dropped with the introduction of Brian&nbsp;
Dennehy's character, Dominic Wilkerson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:26.640
in Season 3. As a former KGB agent posing as&nbsp;
a retired "systems analyst," Dom has a long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:30.300
messy history with Red, one that somehow&nbsp;
intersects with Liz's own background.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:34.140
After a handful of appearances through the&nbsp;
years, Dom becomes integral to the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:39.240
ongoing storyline in Season 7. But producers were&nbsp;
thrown a tragic curveball when Dennehy passed away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:44.400
in April 2020. Rather than rework the upcoming&nbsp;
season's scripts, the powers that be decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:49.020
recast the role with actor Ron Raines, who&nbsp;
played Dom for three episodes in Season 8.

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:52.680
Sometimes a show recasts a role&nbsp;
due to the death of an actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:57.360
but, other times, it's due to the death of the&nbsp;
show itself. That was the case with All Rise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:03.060
the CBS courtroom drama that premiered in 2019, in&nbsp;
which Simone Missick starred as Lola Carmichael,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:06.240
a former prosecutor turned Los&nbsp;
Angeles County's newest judge.

00:08:06.668 --> 00:08:08.820
"I've been in court before.&nbsp;
I know how this works."

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:10.804
"You think you know, but you don't. Not yet."

00:08:10.804 --> 00:08:13.740
The series followed the ins&nbsp;
and outs of her daily caseload,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:17.580
as well as the lives of the men and women who&nbsp;
worked at the courthouse: her fellow judges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:22.500
her old prosecutor colleagues, clerks,&nbsp;
bailiffs, reporters, and more. Lola's husband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:26.280
FBI agent Robin Taylor, was played for&nbsp;
the first two seasons by Todd Williams.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:31.980
The show was canceled by CBS in May 2021.&nbsp;
Several months later, however, it was saved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:37.744
from oblivion by the Oprah Winfrey Network, and&nbsp;
a third and final season aired on OWN in 2022.

00:08:37.935 --> 00:08:43.200
"Instead of&nbsp;&nbsp; asking the cop why I was being ziptied,&nbsp;
you asked me what I did wrong." 

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:48.000
Season 3 took place a year after the events of&nbsp;
Season 2, which saw Lola have a baby and lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.680
her judgeship after defending an innocent woman&nbsp;
from arrest. Most of the original cast returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:56.340
for the final season, with the exception&nbsp;
of Williams, who had booked the Amazon YA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:01.094
series Panic. As result, actor Christian&nbsp;
Keyes took over as Robin for Season 3.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:05.880
Each season of the Breaking Bad prequel,&nbsp;
Better Call Saul, begins with an enigmatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:09.180
black-and-white sequence following the&nbsp;
gray, paranoid life of Jimmy McGill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:13.740
formerly known as New Mexico criminal lawyer&nbsp;
Saul Goodman, now living as Cinnabon manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:17.100
Gene Takovic after the explosive events&nbsp;
of the final season of Breaking Bad.

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:22.500
Starting in Season 3, however, those opening&nbsp;
scenes coalesce into an ongoing story as a panic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.820
attack puts Gene in the hospital. Season 4 begins&nbsp;
with Gene's cab ride back from the hospital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:31.260
where his driver has an Albuquerque Isotopes&nbsp;
decal on his mirror and keeps shooting glances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:35.700
at his passenger. Season 5 reveals that&nbsp;
the driver, Jeff, played by Don Harvey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:39.420
does indeed recognize Gene as Saul,&nbsp;
but his motivations remain unclear.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.320
The sixth and final season forgoes the&nbsp;
black-and-white scenes until the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:47.340
three episodes, where Gene decides not to run&nbsp;
again, but to confront Jeff and neutralize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:52.500
him via an old-fashioned Slippin' Jimmy con game.&nbsp;
For these final episodes, however, Harvey was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:57.360
available due to his commitment to the HBO series&nbsp;
We Own This City. Pat Healy stepped into the role.

00:09:57.701 --> 00:10:00.439
"Jeffie, this is Mr. Takovic."

00:10:00.439 --> 00:10:01.701
"Call me Gene."

00:10:01.903 --> 00:10:06.300
Harvey's original take had a hint of menace,&nbsp;
while Healy's was more of a sad sack divorceé,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:09.600
living with his mother and desperate for&nbsp;
a taste of the Saul Goodman lifestyle.

00:10:10.474 --> 00:10:14.220
Law &amp; Order and its spinoffs have had&nbsp;
no shortage of cast turnover over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:18.300
decades since its 1990 premiere. Part&nbsp;
of that is baked into the premise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:21.540
the show. Detectives and attorneys&nbsp;
come in and out on a regular basis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:25.560
and the departures are explained by&nbsp;
transfer, retirement, arrest, or even death.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:29.580
The eras of the show come to be defined by&nbsp;
who's running the two separate but equally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:33.480
important groups in the criminal justice&nbsp;
system. Seasons 4 through 6 encompass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:36.900
Jill Hennessy's turn as Assistant&nbsp;
District Attorney Claire Kincaid.

00:10:37.034 --> 00:10:40.874
"A search warrant? With&nbsp;
this? You can't be serious."

00:10:40.874 --> 00:10:45.060
Toward the end of Hennessy's run on the show,&nbsp;
she appeared in character as Kincaid on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:49.860
1996 episode of Homicide: Life on the Street.&nbsp;
While it's not exactly a Law &amp; Order spinoff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:53.160
it existed in the same television&nbsp;
universe and occasionally shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:56.760
characters, most memorably Richard&nbsp;
Belzer's Detective John Munch.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:58.860
Filming Homicide on location in Baltimore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:02.700
however, meant that Hennessy missed a few&nbsp;
days of filming the Season 6 Law &amp; Order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:06.944
episode "Corpus Delicti". Luckily, she&nbsp;
had a novel solution to this problem:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.944 --> 00:11:11.924
her identical twin sister Jacqueline could step in&nbsp;
as Kincaid for a few background courtroom shots.

